## Solution

**Pointwise limit.** For each fixed $x>\pi$, as $n\to\infty$ we have $x/n\to 0$, and using $\sin t \sim t$,
$$f_n(x)=\frac{n\sin(x/n)}{x^2}\;\longrightarrow\;\frac{n\cdot(x/n)}{x^2}=\frac{1}{x}.$$
Note $\displaystyle\int_{\pi}^{\infty}\frac{1}{x}\,dx=+\infty$, so the candidate limit is not integrable — a signal that the limit of the integrals should be $+\infty$.

**Reduction by substitution.** Set $u=x/n$, so $x=nu$, $dx=n\,du$, and $x^2=n^2u^2$. The lower limit $x=\pi$ becomes $u=\pi/n$:
$$
I_n:=\int_{\pi}^{\infty}\frac{n\sin(x/n)}{x^2}\,dx
=\int_{\pi/n}^{\infty}\frac{n\sin u}{n^{2}u^{2}}\,n\,du
=\int_{\pi/n}^{\infty}\frac{\sin u}{u^{2}}\,du.
$$
Thus the only $n$-dependence is in the lower limit $\pi/n\to 0^{+}$.

**Splitting the integral.** Write
$$
I_n=\int_{\pi/n}^{1}\frac{\sin u}{u^{2}}\,du+\int_{1}^{\infty}\frac{\sin u}{u^{2}}\,du.
$$
The second term is a fixed constant $C$, since $\left|\frac{\sin u}{u^{2}}\right|\le\frac{1}{u^{2}}$ and $\int_{1}^{\infty}u^{-2}\,du<\infty$ (absolutely convergent).

**The first term diverges to $+\infty$.** For $u\in(0,1]$, using $\sin u\ge u-\frac{u^{3}}{6}\ge\frac{5}{6}u$,
$$
\frac{\sin u}{u^{2}}\ge\frac{5}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{u},
$$
hence
$$
\int_{\pi/n}^{1}\frac{\sin u}{u^{2}}\,du\;\ge\;\frac{5}{6}\int_{\pi/n}^{1}\frac{du}{u}
=\frac{5}{6}\ln\!\left(\frac{n}{\pi}\right)\;\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\;+\infty.
$$
Therefore $I_n\to+\infty$.

**Role of the Dominated Convergence Theorem.** On every bounded subinterval $[\pi,A]$ we have the integrable dominator
$$
|f_n(x)|=\frac{n|\sin(x/n)|}{x^{2}}\le\frac{n\cdot(x/n)}{x^{2}}=\frac{1}{x}\in L^{1}([\pi,A]),
$$
and $f_n\to 1/x$ pointwise, so DCT yields
$$
\int_{\pi}^{A}f_n(x)\,dx\;\longrightarrow\;\int_{\pi}^{A}\frac{dx}{x}=\ln\!\left(\frac{A}{\pi}\right).
$$
Since $\ln(A/\pi)\to+\infty$ as $A\to\infty$, the truncated integrals grow without bound; combined with the substitution analysis above (which shows the oscillatory tail $\int_{1}^{\infty}\frac{\sin u}{u^{2}}du$ contributes only the bounded constant $C$), this confirms that the full integrals $I_n$ tend to $+\infty$.

**Conclusion.**
$$
\boxed{\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\int_{(\pi,\infty)}\frac{n\sin(x/n)}{x^{2}}\,dx=+\infty.}
$$

